{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"curl -L --silent --show-error 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/research/bionlp/RESTful/pmcoa.cgi/BioC_json/31871141/unicode' | head -c 12000\"","aggregated_output":"[{\"bioctype\": \"BioCCollection\", \"source\": \"PMC\", \"date\": \"20260203\", \"key\": \"pmc.key\", \"version\": \"1.0\", \"infons\": {}, \"documents\": [{\"bioctype\": \"BioCDocument\", \"id\": \"PMC7034356\", \"infons\": {\"license\": \"CC BY\"}, \"passages\": [{\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 0, \"infons\": {\"article-id_doi\": \"10.1136/annrheumdis-2019-215926\", \"article-id_pmc\": \"PMC7034356\", \"article-id_pmid\": \"31871141\", \"article-id_publisher-id\": \"annrheumdis-2019-215926\", \"fpage\": \"408\", \"issue\": \"3\", \"kwd\": \"cytokines arthritis fibroblasts\", \"license\": \"This is an open access article distributed in accordance with the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 Unported (CC BY 4.0) license, which permits others to copy, redistribute, remix, transform and build upon this work for any purpose, provided the original work is properly cited, a link to the licence is given, and indication of whether changes were made. See: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/.\", \"lpage\": \"417\", \"name_0\": \"surname:Sun;given-names:Hao\", \"name_1\": \"surname:Wen;given-names:Xingzhao\", \"name_10\": \"surname:Liao;given-names:Weiming\", \"name_11\": \"surname:Zhang;given-names:Zhiqi\", \"name_2\": \"surname:Li;given-names:Hongyi\", \"name_3\": \"surname:Wu;given-names:Peihui\", \"name_4\": \"surname:Gu;given-names:Minghui\", \"name_5\": \"surname:Zhao;given-names:Xiaoyi\", \"name_6\": \"surname:Zhang;given-names:Ziji\", \"name_7\": \"surname:Hu;given-names:Shu\", \"name_8\": \"surname:Mao;given-names:Guping\", \"name_9\": \"surname:Ma;given-names:Ruofan\", \"section_type\": \"TITLE\", \"type\": \"front\", \"volume\": \"79\", \"year\": \"2019\"}, \"text\": \"Single-cell RNA-seq analysis identifies meniscus progenitors and reveals the progression of meniscus degeneration\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 114, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"ABSTRACT\", \"type\": \"abstract_title_1\"}, \"text\": \"Objectives\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 125, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"ABSTRACT\", \"type\": \"abstract\"}, \"text\": \"The heterogeneity of meniscus cells and the mechanism of meniscus degeneration is not well understood. Here, single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) was used to identify various meniscus cell subsets and investigate the mechanism of meniscus degeneration.\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 380, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"ABSTRACT\", \"type\": \"abstract_title_1\"}, \"text\": \"Methods\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 388, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"ABSTRACT\", \"type\": \"abstract\"}, \"text\": \"scRNA-seq was used to identify cell subsets and their gene signatures in healthy human and degenerated meniscus cells to determine their differentiation relationships and characterise the diversity within specific cell types. Colony-forming, multi-differentiation assays and a mice meniscus injury model were used to identify meniscus progenitor cells. We investigated the role of degenerated meniscus progenitor (DegP) cell clusters during meniscus degeneration using computational analysis and experimental verification.\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 911, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"ABSTRACT\", \"type\": \"abstract_title_1\"}, \"text\": \"Results\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 919, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"ABSTRACT\", \"type\": \"abstract\"}, \"text\": \"We identified seven clusters in healthy human meniscus, including five empirically defined populations and two novel populations. Pseudotime analysis showed endothelial cells and fibrochondrocyte progenitors (FCP) existed at the pseudospace trajectory start. Melanoma cell adhesion molecule ((MCAM)/CD146) was highly expressed in two clusters. CD146+ meniscus cells differentiated into osteoblasts and adipocytes and formed colonies. We identified changes in the proportions of degenerated meniscus cell clusters and found a cluster specific to degenerative meniscus with progenitor cell characteristics. The reconstruction of four progenitor cell clusters indicated that FCP differentiation into DegP was an aberrant process. Interleukin 1\\u03b2 stimulation in healthy human meniscus cells increased CD318+ cells, while TGF\\u03b21 attenuated the increase in CD318+ cells in degenerated meniscus cells.\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 1818, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"ABSTRACT\", \"type\": \"abstract_title_1\"}, \"text\": \"Conclusions\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 1830, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"ABSTRACT\", \"type\": \"abstract\"}, \"text\": \"The identification of meniscus progenitor cells provided new insights into cell-based meniscus tissue engineering, demonstrating a novel mechanism of meniscus degeneration, which contributes to the development of a novel therapeutic strategy.\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 2073, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"INTRO\", \"type\": \"title_caption\"}, \"text\": \"Key messages\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 2086, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"INTRO\", \"type\": \"title_1\"}, \"text\": \"What is already known about this subject?\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 2128, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"INTRO\", \"type\": \"paragraph\"}, \"text\": \"The cell types of meniscus contain chondrocyte-like morphology cells and fibroblast-like cells. However, the variety of cell types and corresponding biological markers, as well as the biological targets for the treatment for meniscus degeneration remain elusive.\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 2391, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"INTRO\", \"type\": \"title_1\"}, \"text\": \"What does this study add?\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 2417, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"INTRO\", \"type\": \"paragraph\"}, \"text\": \"This study provides comprehensive census of human meniscus cells using single-cell RNA sequencing, and demonstrating CD146+ meniscus cells are stem/progenitor cells.\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 2583, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"INTRO\", \"type\": \"paragraph\"}, \"text\": \"Interleukin 1\\u03b2 induced activation of degenerated meniscus progenitor cells (DegP) is a potential mechanism contributing to meniscus degeneration.\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 2732, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"CONCL\", \"type\": \"title_1\"}, \"text\": \"How might this impact on clinical practice or future developments?\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 2799, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"CONCL\", \"type\": \"paragraph\"}, \"text\": \"CD146+ meniscus cells have potential in meniscus tissue engineering, and DegP could be a possible therapeutic target for meniscus degeneration.\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 2943, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"INTRO\", \"type\": \"title_1\"}, \"text\": \"Introduction\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 2956, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"INTRO\", \"type\": \"paragraph\"}, \"text\": \"The menisci of mammals are crescent\\u2010shaped tissues, comprised a medial and a lateral component. The meniscus plays an important role in joint stability, shock absorption, distribution of contact forces, joint lubrication and proprioception. The vascularisation of the meniscus decreased with ageing. The meniscus is fully vascularised during prenatal development and shortly after birth, however, only 10%\\u201325% of mature meniscus contains blood vessels. According to these differences in blood supply, the meniscus can be distinguished by the outer vascular region (red zone), inner avascular region (white zone) and the red-white zone between the red and white zones. The outer zone of the meniscus contains 90% type I collagen while the inner zone contains 60% type II collagen and 40% type I collagen. \", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 3765, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"INTRO\", \"type\": \"paragraph\"}, \"text\": \"The cell types of the meniscus are heterogeneous, wherein the inner region contains chondrocyte-like morphology cells and the outer region fibroblast-like cells. Recently, stem/progenitor cells were suggested to be present in the meniscus to promote meniscus injury repair. Gamer et al isolated meniscus stem/progenitor cells by meniscus explant culture in vitro, characterising these cells with clonogenicity properties and abundantly expressed CD44 and Sca-1. However, the cell-type composition and cell distribution in the menisci, as well as biochemical markers for meniscus stem cell/progenitor for use in tissue engineering, remain to be elucidated.\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 4421, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"INTRO\", \"type\": \"paragraph\"}, \"text\": \"The relationship between meniscus degeneration and knee osteoarthritis (OA) is complex. Meniscus degenerative tears were found to be associated with increased cartilage loss in the same compartment, especially in posterior horn tears. Fuller et al found that both inner and outer zone meniscal cells are responsive to the inflammatory cytokines IL-1\\u03b1 and TNF-\\u03b1 in an ovine in vitro model, which leads to cytokine-induced collagenolysis and aggrecanolysis. These studies demonstrated the importance of meniscus degeneration in OA development and its contribution to joint disease in general. Degenerative meniscus accompanied by water content increased the wet weight, while collagen and total glycosaminoglycans (GAG) decreased. However, the variety of cell types and corresponding biological markers, as well as the biological targets for the treatment for meniscus degeneration, have not yet been fully determined.\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 5346, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"INTRO\", \"type\": \"paragraph\"}, \"text\": \"Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) is a well-established and powerful method to investigate transcriptomic cell-to-cell variation, which can be used to identify various cell types and provide insights into physiological and pathological processes. Here, we used scRNA-seq to chart a comprehensive census of meniscus cells. We identified various cell subsets and their gene signatures to determine their differentiation relationships and characterise diversity within specific cell types. We also demonstrated the existence of meniscus stem/progenitor cells and their corresponding marker genes. Finally, we investigated the integral influence of meniscus degeneration on meniscus cellular heterogeneity and identified a potential therapeutic target.\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 6100, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"METHODS\", \"type\": \"title_1\"}, \"text\": \"Materials and methods\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 6122, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"METHODS\", \"type\": \"title_2\"}, \"text\": \"Isolation of human meniscus cells\", \"sentences\": [], \"annotations\": [], \"relations\": []}, {\"bioctype\": \"BioCPassage\", \"offset\": 6156, \"infons\": {\"section_type\": \"METHODS\", \"type\": \"paragraph\"}, \"text\": \"Human meniscus tissues were dissected away from the synovium, and then cut into small pieces. Next, these small pieces were digested by 4\\u2009mg/mL protease (Roche 11459643001) for 1\\u2009hour and 2\\u2009mg/mL collcurl: (23) Failure writing output to destination\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
